{"timestamp":"2026-05-08T14:59:27.522Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"`Unknown` here does **not** mean “transcript could not be parsed.”\n\nIt means the checkpoint row’s stored `agent` field was missing, legacy, or not recognized by `getAgentId()`, so recap buckets it under `unknown`.\n\nSeparately, analysis can still exist because the server has a fallback transcript parser:\n\n- Unknown agent → `FallbackParser`\n- `FallbackParser` delegates to `ClaudeCodeParser`\n- It marks the result as fallback/incomplete, but it can still parse messages/tool calls well enough for labels/tool mix\n- The LLM analysis pipeline then extracts labels like `documentation`, `feature_build`, etc.\n\nSo the confusing state is real but explainable:\n\n```text\nagent attribution: Unknown\ntranscript/analysis: present enough to produce labels/tool metrics\n```\n\nThe actual bug is that recap treats “unknown agent attribution” as a normal top agent and then displays it next to qualitative analysis, which implies a stronger relationship than exists.\n\nI’d fix this in two layers:\n\n1. Server: when `cp.agent` is `Unknown`, derive the recap agent from `cp.sessions[*].agent` if sessions metadata exists. That addresses the root cause for hydrated checkpoints.\n2. CLI: do not show `Unknown` as “top agent” when there are known agents, and make the row labels explicit (`top agent`, `top skill`, `top label`) so `Unknown · documentation` cannot read as “Unknown did documentation.”","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
