{"timestamp":"2026-05-08T18:38:41.153Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"A hydration failure can happen even when the checkpoint/commit exists because the commit row and the transcript payload are separate things.\n\nThe DB may know: “commit X references checkpoint `462f68820c0d`.”\n\nBut hydration still has to fetch the payload from the checkpoint storage ref, usually `entire/checkpoints/v1` or v2:\n\n- root checkpoint metadata\n- per-session metadata\n- transcript files\n\nFor the staged checkpoint we inspected, the API could resolve the checkpoint to a branch, but transcript hydration returned `status=failed` and dashboard-style sessions were empty. That points to “payload unavailable,” not “parser saw transcript and could not classify the agent.”\n\nMain reasons this can happen:\n\n- The checkpoint metadata branch was never pushed, or push failed. User commit got pushed, but `entire/checkpoints/v1` did not.\n- The checkpoint id directory is missing from the checkpoint ref. This can happen with older CLI behavior, skipped hooks, `--no-verify`, failed pre-push, or old/migrated storage.\n- GitHub/App token could not read the checkpoint repo/ref at hydration time.\n- GitHub/go-git-api had a transient read failure: rate limit, timeout, 5xx, auth failure.\n- The metadata exists but references transcript files that are gone or unreadable.\n- The code tried v2 then v1 fallback, or v1 then v2 upgrade, and neither produced usable metadata/transcripts.\n\nImportant: today we only persist coarse state: `pending`, `complete`, `complete_empty`, `error`. We do **not** persist the exact hydration failure reason on the checkpoint row. So for old failed rows, we can say “payload hydration failed,” but not confidently “because GitHub 404” vs “token issue” vs “metadata branch missing” unless we re-run hydration with logging or add persisted error codes.\n\nI think we should add a `last_hydration_error_code` / reason field or equivalent diagnostic so recap can say something precise instead of just “unavailable transcripts.”","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
