{"timestamp":"2026-05-08T18:41:36.959Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"task_complete","turn_id":"019e08e4-d4f6-7af0-aa64-4f9c0281254f","last_agent_message":"Yes, we hydrate on our end, but not from recap today.\n\nCurrent flow:\n\n- **Checkpoint branch push webhook** hydrates immediately.\n  When `entire/checkpoints/v1` or v2 is pushed, the API calls `hydrateCheckpointPayloads(...)`, stores session metadata/transcripts, materializes `repo_sessions`, and dispatches analysis.\n\n- **Normal code branch push webhook** also hydrates when needed.\n  When a commit references checkpoint IDs, the API checks hydration state. If the checkpoint is new, pending, error, or not terminal, it calls `hydrateCheckpointPayloads(...)`.\n\n- **Transcript/detail endpoints lazy-hydrate.**\n  If someone opens a checkpoint transcript and DB has no complete payload, the route schedules background hydration and returns `pending`.\n\n- **But if state is already `error`, transcript route returns `failed` immediately.**\n  It does not retry automatically on every read. That avoids hammering GitHub for permanent failures.\n\n- **Recap does not hydrate.**\n  It reads `repo_checkpoints`, `sessions`, `dead_session_indices`, token fields, and analyses. It does not trigger background hydration or retry failed rows.\n\nSo how we handle it today:\n\n- Fresh/normal paths should hydrate via webhooks.\n- Missing/pending payloads can be repaired lazily by transcript/session reads.\n- Failed payloads are mostly stuck as `error` unless a later webhook/commit sync/admin retry path rehydrates them.\n- Recap currently surfaces the consequences but does not repair them or explain them.\n\nWhat I think we should change:\n\n1. Recap should not count failed/empty live sessions as Unknown sessions.\n2. Recap should include diagnostics: failed/pending/empty transcript counts.\n3. We should add a capped best-effort repair path for pending/missing hydration, but be careful with `error` rows.\n4. We should persist 